  <h2 id="llm">Where this lives in an LLM</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The embedding matrix</strong> is <Math tex="V \times d" /> (50,257 × 768 for GPT-2). Looking up a token is
      reading one row. Equivalently it is a one-hot row vector of length <Math tex="V" /> times the matrix:
      <Math tex="(1 \times V)(V \times d) = 1 \times d" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Every linear layer</strong> is <Math tex="XW^\top + \mathbf{b}" /> on an input of shape
      <Math tex="(\text{batch}, \text{seq}, d)" />. The feed-forward block goes <Math tex="d \to 4d \to d" />, so its
      two weight matrices are <Math tex="4d \times d" /> and <Math tex="d \times 4d" />.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Attention</strong> makes <Math tex="Q" />, <Math tex="K" /> and <Math tex="V" /> with three linear layers,
      forms the <Math tex="n \times n" /> score matrix <Math tex="QK^\top" /> from Fig. 5, and then multiplies the
      softmaxed scores (<Math tex="n \times n" />) by <Math tex="V" /> (<Math tex="n \times d" />) to get
      <Math tex="n \times d" /> again. Three matmuls, and the shape rule tells you the shape at every step.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The KV cache</strong> is literally the <Math tex="K" /> and <Math tex="V" /> matrices of past tokens kept
      in memory so they are not recomputed. Its size is rows × columns × layers × bytes, a shape calculation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Parameter counts</strong> are sums of <Math tex="mn + m" /> over the linear layers, and the
      <strong>2 FLOPs per parameter per token</strong> rule prices a forward pass.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>LoRA</strong> adds <Math tex="BA" /> to a frozen <Math tex="W" />, with <Math tex="B" /> of shape
      <Math tex="d \times r" /> and <Math tex="A" /> of shape <Math tex="r \times d" /> for a small rank <Math tex="r" />,
      and uses associativity to apply it cheaply.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="interview">The full interview answer</h2>
  <p>
    The typical form is a shape question dressed up as something else: “walk me through the shapes in a linear layer
    on a batch” or “what is the shape of the attention scores and why”. Here is a complete answer in about seventy
    seconds.
  </p>
  <Callout kind="interview">
    <p>
      “A linear layer stores a weight matrix of shape (out_features, in_features) and a bias of shape (out_features,).
      For a batch the input is (batch, in_features), and the layer computes <Math tex="XW^\top + \mathbf{b}" />: the
      inner in_features dimensions match and cancel, giving (batch, out_features), with the bias broadcast across the
      rows. Each output entry is the dot product of one example with one output unit’s weight vector. Because matmul
      only touches the last axis, the same layer works on (batch, seq, features) in a transformer.
    </p>
    <p>
      In attention, queries and keys are both (seq, d), and <Math tex="QK^\top" /> is (seq, d)(d, seq) = (seq, seq),
      whose entry <Math tex="(i, j)" /> is the dot product of query <Math tex="i" /> with key <Math tex="j" />: how much
      token <Math tex="i" /> attends to token <Math tex="j" />. Multiplying the softmaxed scores by <Math tex="V" />,
      (seq, seq)(seq, d), gives (seq, d) again. The cost of each matmul is about 2mnp FLOPs, so a forward pass is
      roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per token, and that is why GPUs, which parallelize the independent dot products,
      are the hardware of choice.”
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <p><strong>If they push further, be ready for:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>“Why (out, in) and not (in, out)?” Convention from writing <Math tex="W\mathbf{x}" /> with column vectors; one row per output unit. With row-major batches you apply the transpose.</li>
    <li>“How many parameters in nn.Linear(768, 3072)?” <Math tex="768 \times 3072 + 3072 = 2{,}362{,}368" />.</li>
    <li>“Is matmul commutative?” No. Associative and distributive, yes. Give the LoRA bracketing example.</li>
    <li>“What is the difference between <code>*</code> and <code>@</code>?” Element-wise product versus matrix product.</li>
    <li>“Why is attention quadratic in sequence length?” The score matrix is <Math tex="n \times n" />: <Math tex="n^2 d" /> work and <Math tex="n^2" /> memory.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="questions">Practice questions</h2>
  <p>Answer each one out loud before opening it.</p>
  <QA q="What is the shape of A B if A is 5 × 3 and B is 3 × 7? And B A?">
    <p><Math tex="AB" /> is <Math tex="5 \times 7" />. <Math tex="BA" /> is <Math tex="(3 \times 7)(5 \times 3)" />: inner 7 and 5 do not match, so it is not defined.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="In one sentence, what is entry (i, j) of A B?">
    <p>The dot product of row <Math tex="i" /> of <Math tex="A" /> with column <Math tex="j" /> of <Math tex="B" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What are the shapes of the weight and bias of nn.Linear(in_features=20, out_features=5), and how many parameters does it have?">
    <p>Weight <code>(5, 20)</code>, bias <code>(5,)</code>. Parameters <Math tex="5 \times 20 + 5 = 105" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="A batch tensor has shape (16, 50, 20) and goes through nn.Linear(20, 5). What comes out?">
    <p><code>(16, 50, 5)</code>. Only the last axis changes.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Why does a linear layer compute X Wᵀ rather than X W?">
    <p>Because <Math tex="W" /> is stored as (out, in) with one row per output unit, and the batch <Math tex="X" /> is (batch, in). Row-with-row dot products need the second matrix flipped so its rows become columns: <Math tex="(\text{batch}, \text{in})(\text{in}, \text{out})" />.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Is matrix multiplication commutative? Associative? Give one practical consequence of each answer.">
    <p>Not commutative: swapping changes the result or the shape, so formulas must keep their order. Associative: you may choose the bracketing, so LoRA computes <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" /> instead of <Math tex="(BA)\mathbf{x}" /> and saves hundreds of times the work.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is (A B)ᵀ?">
    <p><Math tex="B^\top A^\top" />. The order reverses; check with shapes.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Q and K are both (n, d). What is Q Kᵀ, what is its entry (i, j), and why is attention called quadratic?">
    <p><Math tex="n \times n" />; entry <Math tex="(i, j)" /> is <Math tex="\mathbf{q}_i \cdot \mathbf{k}_j" />; there are <Math tex="n^2" /> entries, each costing <Math tex="d" /> operations, so time and memory grow with the square of the sequence length.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="How many FLOPs is a matmul of (m × n) by (n × p), and what rule of thumb follows for a forward pass?">
    <p>About <Math tex="2mnp" />. A weight matrix with <Math tex="mn" /> parameters costs <Math tex="2mn" /> FLOPs per input vector, so a forward pass is roughly 2 FLOPs per parameter per token.</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What is the difference between A * B and A @ B in NumPy?">
    <p><code>A * B</code> multiplies matching entries (element-wise, same shape in or out). <code>A @ B</code> is matrix multiplication (dot products of rows with columns, shapes must chain).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="Can you add an array of shape (4,) to one of shape (4, 2)? What about (2,)?">
    <p>(4,) fails: aligned from the right, 4 is compared with 2. (2,) works: it is broadcast as one copy per row. To add per-row values reshape to (4, 1).</p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="What do the columns of a matrix tell you about the function x ↦ M x?">
    <p>They are where the basis vectors land. Column 1 is <Math tex="M" /> applied to (1, 0, …), column 2 to (0, 1, …), and every other input goes to the corresponding linear combination of the columns.</p>
  </QA>

  <h2 id="practice">Practice problems</h2>
  <p>Paper first. Write shapes before numbers, every time. Solutions are hidden under each problem.</p>
  <h3>By hand</h3>
  <QA q="1. For each pair of shapes, say whether A B is defined and give its shape: (2,3)(3,4); (3,4)(2,3); (1,5)(5,1); (5,1)(1,5); (4,4)(4,4); (6,2)(2,2).">
    <p>
      (2,4); not defined (4 ≠ 2); (1,1), a single number; (5,5); (4,4); (6,2).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="2. Compute A B and B A for A = [[1, 2], [3, 4]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 2]].">
    <p>
      <Math tex="AB" />: row 1 of A with columns of B: <Math tex="(2 + 2, 0 + 4) = (4, 4)" />; row 2: <Math tex="(6 + 4, 0 + 8) = (10, 8)" />.
      So <Math tex="AB = [[4, 4], [10, 8]]" />. <Math tex="BA" />: row 1 of B: <Math tex="(2 + 0, 4 + 0) = (2, 4)" />; row 2: <Math tex="(1 + 6, 2 + 8) = (7, 10)" />.
      So <Math tex="BA = [[2, 4], [7, 10]]" />. Different, as expected.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="3. Multiply A = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] by the column (1, 0, −1). Then read the answer as a combination of A’s columns.">
    <p>
      Row 1: <Math tex="1 + 0 - 3 = -2" />; row 2: <Math tex="4 + 0 - 6 = -2" />. Result <Math tex="(-2, -2)" />.
      As columns: <Math tex="1 \cdot (1, 4) + 0 \cdot (2, 5) - 1 \cdot (3, 6) = (-2, -2)" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="4. Write the transpose of [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]] and of the column vector (7, 8, 9). What are their shapes?">
    <p>
      <Math tex="[[1, 4], [2, 5], [3, 6]]" />, shape 3 × 2. The column (3 × 1) becomes the row <Math tex="(7, 8, 9)" />, shape 1 × 3.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="5. Three walk days X = [[1,1,1], [0,1,0], [1,0,0]], weights W = [[3,−2,−4],[1,−1,0]], bias b = (−1, 0). Compute Z = X Wᵀ + b with its shape.">
    <p>
      Shape (3 × 3)(3 × 2) = 3 × 2. Day 1 (1,1,1): neuron 1: <Math tex="3 - 2 - 4 - 1 = -4" />; neuron 2: <Math tex="1 - 1 + 0 = 0" />.
      Day 2 (0,1,0): <Math tex="-2 - 1 = -3" />; <Math tex="-1" />. Day 3 (1,0,0): <Math tex="3 - 1 = 2" />; <Math tex="1" />.
      <Math tex="Z = [[-4, 0], [-3, -1], [2, 1]]" />.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="6. A network has layers nn.Linear(784, 256), nn.Linear(256, 128), nn.Linear(128, 10). Give every weight shape and the total parameter count.">
    <p>
      Weights (256, 784), (128, 256), (10, 128). Parameters: <Math tex="784 \cdot 256 + 256 = 200{,}960" />;
      <Math tex="256 \cdot 128 + 128 = 32{,}896" />; <Math tex="128 \cdot 10 + 10 = 1{,}290" />. Total 235,146.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="7. Input (64, 784) through the network in problem 6. Write the shape after each layer. Then do it for an input of shape (8, 20, 784).">
    <p>
      (64, 256) → (64, 128) → (64, 10). With the extra axis: (8, 20, 256) → (8, 20, 128) → (8, 20, 10). Only the last axis changes.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="8. Q and K are (128, 64). Give the shape of Q Kᵀ, the number of entries, and the FLOPs to compute it. If the sequence doubles to 256, by what factor does each grow?">
    <p>
      (128, 128); 16,384 entries; about <Math tex="2 \cdot 128 \cdot 64 \cdot 128 \approx 2.1" /> million FLOPs. Doubling
      the sequence quadruples the entries (65,536) and the FLOPs (about 8.4 million). Quadratic in sequence length.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="9. LoRA with d = 1024 and rank r = 16. How many parameters do B (d × r) and A (r × d) hold together, as a percentage of the full d × d matrix? Compare the cost of (B A) x against B (A x) for one vector.">
    <p>
      <Math tex="2 \cdot 1024 \cdot 16 = 32{,}768" /> parameters against <Math tex="1024^2 = 1{,}048{,}576" />: about 3.1%.
      Forming <Math tex="BA" /> first costs <Math tex="1024 \cdot 16 \cdot 1024 \approx 16.8" /> million multiplications;
      <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" /> costs <Math tex="16 \cdot 1024 + 1024 \cdot 16 = 32{,}768" />. About 500 times cheaper.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="10. Which of these broadcasts, and to what shape? (3, 4) + (4,); (3, 4) + (3,); (3, 4) + (3, 1); (3, 1) + (1, 4); (2, 3, 4) + (3, 4); (2, 3, 4) + (2, 4).">
    <p>
      (3, 4) yes; (3,) no (3 vs 4); (3, 1) yes → (3, 4); (3, 1)+(1, 4) yes → (3, 4), both stretch; (2, 3, 4)+(3, 4) yes → (2, 3, 4); (2, 4) no (4 vs 4 fine, then 2 vs 3 fails).
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="11. M = [[0, −1], [1, 0]]. Where do (1, 0) and (0, 1) go? Sketch the image of the unit square. What does this matrix do to the plane?">
    <p>
      Columns: (1, 0) → (0, 1) and (0, 1) → (−1, 0). The square turns a quarter turn anticlockwise. <Math tex="M" /> is a
      90° rotation. Apply it twice: <Math tex="M^2 = [[-1, 0], [0, -1]]" />, a half turn.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <QA q="12. GPT-2 small: d = 768, vocabulary 50,257, 12 layers. Estimate the parameters in the embedding matrix and in the two feed-forward matrices of one layer (768 → 3072 → 768). Then estimate forward-pass FLOPs per token for the feed-forward parts of all 12 layers.">
    <p>
      Embedding: <Math tex="50{,}257 \times 768 \approx 38.6" /> million. Feed-forward per layer:
      <Math tex="768 \cdot 3072 + 3072 + 3072 \cdot 768 + 768 \approx 4.72" /> million. All 12: about 56.7 million.
      FLOPs per token: 2 per parameter, about 113 million for the feed-forward blocks alone.
    </p>
  </QA>
  <h3>Code</h3>
  <QA q="13. Implement matmul with three nested loops. Check it against @ on random shapes, and make it raise a clear error when the inner dimensions differ.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

def matmul(A, B):
    m, n = A.shape
    n2, p = B.shape
    if n != n2:
        raise ValueError(f"inner dimensions differ: A is {m}x{n}, B is {n2}x{p}")
    C = np.zeros((m, p))
    for i in range(m):              # each row of A
        for j in range(p):          # each column of B
            for k in range(n):      # the dot product
                C[i, j] += A[i, k] * B[k, j]
    return C

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
for m, n, p in [(2, 3, 2), (5, 1, 4), (3, 7, 3), (1, 6, 1)]:
    A, B = rng.normal(size=(m, n)), rng.normal(size=(n, p))
    assert np.allclose(matmul(A, B), A @ B)
try:
    matmul(np.zeros((2, 3)), np.zeros((2, 3)))
except ValueError as e:
    print(e)                        # inner dimensions differ: A is 2x3, B is 2x3
print("ok")`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="14. Time your loop matmul against NumPy’s @ for 200 × 200 matrices. Then time @ for 1000 × 1000. Compute the FLOPs per second NumPy achieves.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np, time

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
A, B = rng.normal(size=(200, 200)), rng.normal(size=(200, 200))

t = time.perf_counter(); C1 = matmul(A, B); loop_s = time.perf_counter() - t      # uses matmul from problem 13
t = time.perf_counter(); C2 = A @ B;       numpy_s = time.perf_counter() - t
assert np.allclose(C1, C2)
print(f"loops {loop_s:.2f}s   numpy {numpy_s*1000:.2f}ms   speed-up {loop_s/numpy_s:,.0f}x")

n = 1000
A, B = rng.normal(size=(n, n)), rng.normal(size=(n, n))
t = time.perf_counter(); A @ B; s = time.perf_counter() - t
flops = 2 * n * n * n
print(f"{flops/s/1e9:.1f} GFLOP/s on this CPU")   # tens to hundreds; a GPU does tens of thousands`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="15. Write a two-layer network forward pass in NumPy for a batch, asserting the shape after every step. Then make it accept an input with an extra leading axis without changing any code.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
W1, b1 = rng.normal(size=(4, 3)) * 0.5, np.zeros(4)      # (out, in)
W2, b2 = rng.normal(size=(2, 4)) * 0.5, np.zeros(2)

def forward(X):
    H = X @ W1.T + b1;      assert H.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (4,)
    H = np.maximum(H, 0);   assert H.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (4,)
    Y = H @ W2.T + b2;      assert Y.shape == X.shape[:-1] + (2,)
    return Y

print(forward(rng.normal(size=(32, 3))).shape)        # (32, 2)
print(forward(rng.normal(size=(8, 128, 3))).shape)    # (8, 128, 2)  same code, extra leading axis`}
    />
  </QA>
  <QA q="16. Compute all pairwise cosine similarities between 1,000 random 64-dimensional embeddings in one matrix expression. Verify one entry by hand with the dot product formula, and confirm the diagonal is all ones.">
    <CodeBlock
      code={`import numpy as np

rng = np.random.default_rng(0)
E = rng.normal(size=(1000, 64))
E_unit = E / np.linalg.norm(E, axis=1, keepdims=True)    # (1000, 64) / (1000, 1): broadcasting
S = E_unit @ E_unit.T                                    # (1000, 1000): every pair at once

i, j = 3, 17
by_hand = (E[i] @ E[j]) / (np.linalg.norm(E[i]) * np.linalg.norm(E[j]))
assert np.isclose(S[i, j], by_hand)
assert np.allclose(np.diag(S), 1.0)                      # every vector is perfectly similar to itself
assert np.allclose(S, S.T)                               # similarity is symmetric
print(S.shape, S[i, j])`}
    />
  </QA>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Common mistakes</h2>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Using <code>*</code> for matrix multiplication.</strong> It is element-wise. For two square matrices it
      runs without error and returns the wrong thing. Matmul is <code>@</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Reading shapes as (columns, rows).</strong> Shape is always (rows, columns), and a linear layer’s weight
      is (out, in). If a shape error names dimensions that “should” match, check which axis is which before anything
      else.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Mixing (n,), (n, 1) and (1, n).</strong> <code>x @ x</code> is a scalar for shape (n,), but a (n, 1) times
      (n, 1) is an error and a (1, n) times (n, 1) is a (1, 1) matrix. When something is “almost” a number but prints
      with brackets, this is why. Use <code>.reshape</code> or <code>[:, None]</code> deliberately.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Broadcasting a per-row vector without adding an axis.</strong> <code>Z - Z.mean(axis=1)</code> fails for
      a non-square <code>Z</code> and silently does the wrong thing for a square one. Use
      <code>keepdims=True</code>.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Assuming <Math tex="AB = BA" />.</strong> Order carries meaning: which side you multiply on decides
      whether you act on rows or columns. Keep formulas in their written order.
    </p>
  </Callout>
  <Callout kind="pitfall">
    <p>
      <strong>Materializing a low-rank product.</strong> Computing <Math tex="BA" /> as a full matrix when you only
      need <Math tex="BA\mathbf{x}" /> throws away the whole point of the factorization. Bracket as
      <Math tex="B(A\mathbf{x})" />.
    </p>
  </Callout>

